<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.13.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00615</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.13.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5cb512b7-de8f-4077-8e33-6d393b860e86</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="d9e6701c-63d6-41fa-bc53-e04b99537fae" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><quote>This year, students in the 9th grade are collecting dimes and quarters for a school fundraiser. They are trying to collect more money than the students who were in the 9th grade last year. The students in 9th grade last year collected $143.88.</quote></para><para>Using \(d\) to represent the number of dimes collected and \(q\) to represent the number of quarters, which statement best represents this situation?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(0.25d + 0.1q ≥ 143.88\)</para><para>\(0.25q + 0.1d &lt; 143.88\)</para><para>\(0.25d + 0.1q &gt; 143.88\)</para><para>\(0.25q + 0.1d &gt; 143.88\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The value of the quarters, \(0.25q\), plus the value of the dimes, \(0.1d\), is more than $143.88.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(0.25q + 0.1d &gt;143.88\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="09b59ea2-8e47-4950-b93c-3d3c27fe25be" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>Which inequality is represented by the graph?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(4x − 2y &gt; 12\)</para><para>\(4x − 2y &lt; 12\)</para><para>\(4x + 2y &gt; 12\)</para><para>\(4x + 2y &lt; 12\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The graph shows a dashed boundary line for the related equation \(y = 2x - 6\) and the shading is above the line. </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(4x - 2y \lt 12\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="56fa3e61-916e-436f-bd1e-361b975b8baa" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For 3 - 4, use the passage below to answer the questions:</para><para><quote> A farmer is creating a budget for planting soybeans and wheat. Planting soybeans costs $200 per acre and planting wheat costs $500 per acre. He wants to spend no more than $100,000 planting soybeans and wheat.</quote></para><para>Let \(s\) represent the number of acres of soybeans and \(w\) represent the number of acres of wheat.
          Write an inequality to describe the situation.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(500s + 200w &lt; 100,000\)</para><para>\(500s + 200w ≤ 100,000\)</para><para>\(200s + 500w ≤ 100,000\)</para><para>\(200s + 500w &gt; 100,000\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The soybeans cost 200s dollars and the wheat costs \(500w\). The sum must be less than or equal to $100,000.
      </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(200s + 500w ≤ 100,000\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="af1ef011-ce52-496e-a059-5924974c7d45" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Name one solution to
          the inequality and explain what it represents in that situation.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>(200, 130)</para><para>(250, 100)</para><para>(170, 140)</para><para>(300, 90)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(200(250) + 500(100) = 100,000\), which is the maximum of the farmer’s budget.
      </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3.</para><para>One possible correct answer is \((250, 100)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="48553143-b120-4bb4-bfc3-e5c7c5aa7f53" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For 5 - 7, use the passage below to answer the questions:</para><para><quote> Amira is ordering dried chili peppers and corn husks for her cooking class. Chili peppers cost $16.95 per pound and corn husks cost $6.49 per pound.</quote><quote> Amira spends less than $50 on \(d\) pounds of dried chili peppers and \(h\) pounds of corn husks.</quote><quote>Here is a graph that represents this situation. </quote></para><para>Write an
          inequality that represents this situation.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(16.95d + 6.49h ≥ 50\)</para><para>\(16.95d – 6.49h &lt; 50\)</para><para>\(16.95d + 6.49h &lt; 50\)</para><para>\(16.95d + 6.49h &gt; 50\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The cost of dried chili peppers, \(16.95d\), plus the cost of corn husks, 6.49h, must be less than $50.
      </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(16.95d + 6.49h &lt; 50\). </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="87928af0-7213-40e9-8c7f-ec5beaaeb638" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Can Amira purchase 2 pounds of dried chili peppers and 4 pounds of corn husks
          and spend less than $50? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No. She would be spending \(16.95(2) + 6.49(4)\), or $59.86.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3.</para><para>The correct answer is No. She would be spending \(16.95(2) + 6.49(4)\), or $59.86. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="8fa1b4ce-573c-418c-8d99-7cf6deb543a9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Can Amira purchase 1.5 pounds of dried chili peppers and 3 pounds of corn
          husks and spend less than $50? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Yes. She would be spending \(16.95(1.5) + 6.49(3)\), or $44.90.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3. </para><para>The correct answer is Yes. She would be spending \(16.95(1.5) + 6.49(3)\), or $44.90. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="a9937132-55c8-4ecf-ac34-62aa3212d11e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For 8 - 10, use the passage below to answer the questions:</para><para><quote>A beach store rents surfboards for $15 per hour and beach umbrellas for $10 per hour. The store wants to make at least $200 per day on renting beach equipment. Let \(s\) be the number of hours that a surfboard is rented and \(u\) be the number of hours an umbrella is rented.</quote></para><para>Which inequality represents this situation?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(15s – 10u ≥ 200\)</para><para>\(15s + 10u ≤ 200\)</para><para>\(15s + 10u ≥ 200\)</para><para>\(15s + 10u &lt; 200\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! The store makes \(15s\) dollars per hour renting surfboards and \(10u\) dollars renting umbrellas. The sum must be greater than or equal to $200.
      </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(15s + 10u ≥ 200\). </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="be434d8c-84fb-4e60-aeb5-f281d2c0b5fc" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Can the
          beach store rent surfboards for 7 total hours and rent umbrellas for 9
          total hours and reach their goal of making $200? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No.They would be making \(15(7) + 10(9)\), or $195.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3. </para><para>The correct answer is No. They would be making \(15(7) + 10(9)\), or $195. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="22ae87a4-6ce0-4a0f-92df-0a05c8da3e41" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Can the
          beach store rent surfboards for 10 total hours and rent umbrellas for 4
          total hours and reach their goal of making $200? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No.They would be making \(15(10) + 10(4)\), or \($190\).</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 2.13.2 and 2.13.3.</para><para>The correct answer is No. They would be making \(15(10) + 10(4)\), or $190. </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="112ec7bb-6ccc-4e5d-a667-9e7644f57e8d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Mariah has data in a table. Use desmos to plot the data and find a possible inequality in two variables that could be represented by this data. Which graph represents this inequality?</para>
      <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
<thead>
         <row>
           <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
       <tbody>
         <row>
           <entry>−4</entry>
           <entry>7</entry>
         </row>
         <row>
           <entry>−2</entry>
           <entry>3</entry>
         </row>
         <row>
           <entry>0</entry>
           <entry>-1</entry>
         </row>
         <row>
           <entry>2</entry>
           <entry>-5</entry>
         </row>
       </tbody>
     </table>
        
    <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y &lt; -2x-4\)</para><para>\(y \leq -2x-4\)</para><para>\(y &gt; -2x-4\)</para><para>\(y \leq -2x-4\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y &gt; -2x-4\)</para><para>Try again. </para><para>The correct answer is \(y &gt; -2x-4\)</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
